$OctSevenArticles = @(
  @{
    Slug = 'jar-lid-tamper-seals-vs-shrink-bands'
    Title = 'Jar Lid Tamper Seals vs Shrink Bands: Which Fits Your Pack?'
    Description = 'Compare printed jar lid tamper seals with shrink bands by opening evidence, application workflow, branding area and finished-pack testing.'
    Category = 'Package opening evidence'
    CardDescription = 'A narrow printed seal and a full neck band solve different packing problems.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of two unbranded illustrated jam jars, one with a complete printed lid-bridge seal and one with a transparent shrink band'
    Caption = 'A printed bridge seal and a shrink band should be compared on the actual closed jar.'
    Published = '2026-10-07'
    Faq = @(
      @('Is every jar lid sticker tamper evident?', 'No. A decorative closure sticker may peel away without clear evidence. Define the visible change and test it on the finished jar before making that claim.'),
      @('Does a shrink band replace a printed label?', 'Not necessarily. A band can provide a different opening indicator, while a printed label can carry product identity. The packaging owner should assess both functions and any applicable rules.'),
      @('Can one tamper seal fit glass and plastic jars?', 'Do not assume so. Lid finish, jar surface, edge geometry and opening force can change the result. Trial each package construction separately.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>Picture a row of filled jars waiting for a final closure check. A slim illustrated strip bridges the lid and glass on one jar; a clear band wraps the cap and neck on the next. Both may look reassuring in a product photo. The useful question is simpler: what changes when someone opens this exact pack?</p>
  <p>I would choose the closure only after watching the normal opening motion. A pressure-sensitive bridge label can connect two surfaces and carry original artwork. A shrink band surrounds more of the closure, but needs a different application and shrinking process. Neither is automatically right for every jar, and a label printer should not claim to supply or certify a band it has not specified.</p>
  <h2>Compare the Opening Evidence, Not the Graphic</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Printed bridge seal</th><th>Shrink band</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where it sits</td><td>Across lid edge and jar body</td><td>Around cap and neck</td></tr><tr><td>Branding space</td><td>Small printed surface for an original mark</td><td>Coverage and print options depend on band design</td></tr><tr><td>Line setup</td><td>Placement and pressure across two surfaces</td><td>Band placement and controlled shrink operation</td></tr><tr><td>Opening check</td><td>Look for tear, split or transfer as specified</td><td>Look for the intended break or removal evidence</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>The table is a planning comparison, not a performance promise. A plain paper strip that lifts intact may be only a decorative closure. A poorly fitted band may not give the intended result either. For regulated goods, the product owner must confirm the required package feature and labeling statement with qualified advisers. The <a href="https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics/cosmetics-labeling-regulations/summary-cosmetics-labeling-requirements" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FDA's cosmetics labeling summary</a> illustrates why a visible indicator and its accompanying statement cannot be inferred from artwork alone.</p>
  <h2>Give the Seal a Job and a Place</h2>
  <p>If the jar label is primarily a small branded closure, an original botanical illustration or a clear batch symbol can make it feel deliberate. Keep any safety-looking icon honest. Do not add a fake approval mark. Mark the exact landing area on lid and body; metal, coated plastic and glass can ask different things of the adhesive. If a band is preferred, confirm cap diameter, neck shape and equipment with the band supplier rather than reusing sticker dimensions.</p>
  <h2>Run a Finished-Jar Trial</h2>
  <p>Close representative filled jars using the normal process. Apply the proposed feature, let it pass through packing and storage conditions, then open it as a customer would. Photograph both the unopened and opened result. Repeat on each lid and jar family. The most valuable sample is the one that shows an inconvenient failure before a bulk run.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Identify the actual first opening movement</li><li>State the visible evidence expected</li><li>Confirm lid, jar and surface finishes</li><li>Trial application on packed jars</li><li>Inspect after storage and shipping handling</li><li>Have the product owner approve any regulated wording</li></ul>
  <p>Read the <a href="/blog/tamper-evident-labels-guide/">tamper-evident label guide</a> for construction questions and the <a href="/blog/custom-jam-jar-labels-guide/">jam jar label guide</a> for sticky filling and artwork planning. Send a closed-jar photo, opening video and desired evidence through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The right choice is the feature that works on the pack, not just in the mockup.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-squeeze-tube-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Squeeze Tube Labels: Make Flex, Seams and Wet Use Work'
    Description = 'Plan custom labels for cosmetic and hand-cream squeeze tubes around flexible walls, usable panels, seam placement, wet handling and sample trials.'
    Category = 'Flexible tube packaging'
    CardDescription = 'A tube label should be judged after squeezing, not only on a flat proof.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original illustrated hand-cream labels applied to flexible unbranded squeeze tubes with visible seam clearance'
    Caption = 'Proof the decorated tube in a hand, after a squeeze, with the seam visible.'
    Published = '2026-10-07'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can any sticker go on a squeeze tube?', 'No. Tube flexibility, coating, contents, application method and label construction all affect performance. Trial the intended material on the filled tube.'),
      @('Where should the label seam go?', 'Keep the seam away from the most compressed and visible area when the tube design allows. Confirm placement with a wrapped physical sample.'),
      @('Should a squeeze tube be directly printed instead?', 'Sometimes direct decoration is the better route, especially when a label edge or repeated flex creates unacceptable risk. Compare process, artwork changes and volume before deciding.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A squeeze tube can look like a tiny bottle in a catalog image. In use it folds, bows and gets handled with damp or lotion-covered fingers. That is why I would never approve the label from a flat proof alone. Put it on a filled tube, squeeze it the way a customer will, and inspect the edge afterward.</p>
  <p>First separate three choices: a front label, a wrap label, or direct tube decoration. Labels can help with variant changes and detailed artwork, but the seam and edges need a sensible place to live. Direct print may avoid a separate edge entirely. It deserves a fair comparison rather than being dismissed because the project started as a sticker inquiry.</p>
  <h2>Find the Panel That Actually Stays Flat</h2>
  <p>Measure between the shoulder and the crimp on the final tube, not a generic cylinder drawing. Note where the cap, seal and back seam sit. Wrap a paper prototype around the filled package. The longest product name, ingredient panel and lot-code space should fit without crossing the area that creases hardest.</p>
  <h2>Test the Squeeze, Then the Bathroom</h2>
  <p>A proposed label film may be designed for conformability, but material family alone cannot guarantee the result on a particular tube. The <a href="https://label.averydennison.com/na/en/home/products/film/conformable-film.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Avery Dennison conformable-film overview</a> is a useful starting point for the material conversation, not a substitute for a sample.</p>
  <p>Apply labels through the intended packing workflow. Squeeze the tube repeatedly, set it down wet, wipe away controlled contact with the actual formula and inspect print, finish and adhesion after the planned dwell. Do not quietly substitute water for oil or lotion when the product can touch the label. If a label edge becomes the failure point, move it, change construction or reconsider direct decoration.</p>
  <h2>Let the Original Art Help Identification</h2>
  <p>An illustrated rosemary sprig, citrus slice or colored stripe can distinguish two hand-cream variants without imitating another brand. Keep the product-type word readable at shelf distance. Small mandatory text and claims belong to the responsible brand's review; a printer can flag space and reproduction concerns but should not invent approved wording.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send filled tubes and exact material information</li><li>Mark shoulder, crimp and squeeze zones</li><li>Compare front label, wrap and direct print</li><li>Trial repeated flex and wet handling</li><li>Test contact with the actual formula</li><li>Approve longest SKU name and code area</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-shampoo-bottle-labels-guide/">shampoo bottle guide</a> covers wet-use questions; the <a href="/blog/custom-cosmetic-labels-guide/">cosmetic label guide</a> covers small text and package curves. Send a tube photo and expected use to the <a href="/get-quote/">quote form</a>. I would rather lose an attractive mockup than deliver a label whose edge lifts halfway through the tube.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'retail-barcode-labels-for-product-packaging'
    Title = 'Retail Barcode Labels: Scan the Finished Pack, Not the Proof'
    Description = 'Plan printed retail barcode labels with approved product IDs, quiet zones, contrast, package placement and scan tests on final packaging.'
    Category = 'Retail product identification'
    CardDescription = 'A barcode is useful only when the approved code can be read on the sold pack.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of fully printed original botanical retail product labels with a clean barcode panel being scanned on unbranded cartons'
    Caption = 'Keep the design, but reserve a clean panel for the approved retail code.'
    Published = '2026-10-07'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can the printer create my retail product number?', 'The brand or authorized data owner must supply the approved product identifier and barcode specification. The printer should not invent retail IDs.'),
      @('Can a barcode sit on a curved bottle?', 'It can be proposed, but curvature and placement affect scanning. Use the applicable GS1 guidance and test the code on the filled retail pack.'),
      @('Will a phone scan prove a retail barcode is acceptable?', 'No. A phone test is a useful quick check, not a substitute for the retailer-specific scanner and formal verification required by the project.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A barcode can look razor sharp in the design file and still be the weakest part of a product label. A box fold cuts into the white margin, a dark illustration creeps behind the bars, or the bottle curve turns the symbol away from the scanner. I would start with the real checkout or receiving path: who scans it, on which finished package, and from what side?</p>
  <p>The brand owns the approved product identifier and the decision about which code belongs on each variant. The label supplier can reproduce that data and check print placement, but should not invent a GTIN, substitute a sample number or silently reuse one SKU's barcode on another.</p>
  <h2>Protect the Quiet Zone Before Styling the Label</h2>
  <p>GS1 identifies quiet zones, symbol size, contrast and placement as common barcode quality checks in its <a href="https://support.gs1.org/support/solutions/articles/43000734141-what-should-i-check-to-ensure-good-quality-barcodes-" rel="noopener" target="_blank">barcode quality guidance</a>. Treat the white area around the bars as part of the code, not empty space available for a border or illustration. A decorative frame that touches it can make a good design a bad scan.</p>
  <p>Print a proof at final size on the intended material. Check whether laminate glare, a textured carton, a dark bottle or a clear label changes the contrast. Put the label on the filled package and try the scanner used in the actual workflow. A successful phone-camera read is helpful, but it does not replace any formal verification the retailer requests.</p>
  <h2>Make Variant Control Boring on Purpose</h2>
  <p>For a range of flavors or scents, pair each original illustrated front label with the correct approved identifier on the back or side. Use a short human-readable SKU name as a second check. When artwork changes, reconcile the approved data file, proof version and carton label before the next run. It is much easier to catch a swapped code at proof stage than after products reach a shelf.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Evidence to request</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Which code belongs to this SKU?</td><td>Owner-approved data list</td></tr><tr><td>Can it scan on this package?</td><td>Final-size printed and applied sample</td></tr><tr><td>Does the design interfere?</td><td>Clear quiet zone and contrast check</td></tr><tr><td>Will reorders stay matched?</td><td>Versioned proof and code record</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>A barcode label need not be a dull white rectangle. Give the product a complete original visual identity, then let the code occupy its own disciplined space. The <a href="/blog/qr-code-stickers-scan-testing-guide/">QR code guide</a> covers a different symbol and use case; the <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-reorder-checklist/">reorder checklist</a> helps protect SKU versions. Send the approved data and a packed-product photo through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'cold-brew-bottle-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Cold Brew Bottle Labels: Condensation, Curves and Batch Dates'
    Description = 'Plan custom cold brew bottle labels around chilled glass or PET, condensation, front-panel size, batch coding and filled-bottle trials.'
    Category = 'Chilled beverage packaging'
    CardDescription = 'The bottle looks different after a trip from the refrigerator to a warm counter.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of chilled unbranded cold brew bottles with complete original coffee-bean illustrated labels and visible condensation'
    Caption = 'Judge the label on a cold, filled bottle after condensation develops.'
    Published = '2026-10-07'
    Faq = @(
      @('Are paper labels unsuitable for every cold brew bottle?', 'No universal answer is responsible. Review the material, finish, adhesive, condensation, storage and service conditions with physical filled-bottle samples.'),
      @('Should batch dates be printed on every label in advance?', 'That depends on the bottling workflow. The brand should choose an approved variable-data method and reserve a legible code area before the artwork is finalized.'),
      @('Can one label fit glass and PET versions?', 'Only after measuring and testing each bottle. Shape, surface and application behavior can differ even when nominal volume matches.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A cold brew bottle has two lives. One is the dry studio shot. The other begins when a cold bottle is set on a warm counter and water beads across the front. I would approve the label in the second life. If the name disappears under glare or an edge starts to lift there, the first photo has not solved the buyer's problem.</p>
  <p>Start by deciding whether the product ships in glass, PET or both. Ask for filled, capped samples from the actual bottle supplier. The straight printable panel, shoulder curve and seam can change between bottles that share the same stated volume. A wrap intended for one may wrinkle on another.</p>
  <h2>Separate Chilling From Application</h2>
  <p>Where is the label applied: before refrigeration, to a room-temperature filled bottle, or after the bottle is already cold? That sequence matters to the trial. Do not call a material "freezer grade" because the drink is chilled, and do not promise water resistance from a film name alone. Trial the proposed face, finish and adhesive on the bottle through the actual application and storage cycle.</p>
  <h2>Design Around a Damp Hand</h2>
  <p>An original coffee-bean illustration, a clear roast name and a restrained color system can separate classic, oat and decaf variants. The artwork should survive a wet thumb across the front and remain readable when droplets collect. Try the label under cooler lighting and after wiping it with the hand motion a buyer would use. Keep batch and date coding away from deep curves or condensation-prone folds.</p>
  <p>If a removable promotional sticker is added for a launch, test it separately from the permanent product label. The extra piece should not cover approved product information or interfere with the main barcode. The brand remains responsible for ingredient, nutrition and market-specific claims; a printer can help reserve legible space.</p>
  <h2>Run a Small Finished-Bottle Check</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Measure filled glass and PET bottles separately</li><li>Record application temperature and sequence</li><li>Check condensation after normal refrigeration</li><li>Rub the print and inspect edge lift</li><li>Proof every flavor and batch-code position</li><li>Scan any retail code on the wet finished pack</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-roll-labels-for-bottles-and-jars/">bottle roll-label guide</a> helps with line format, and the <a href="/blog/custom-coffee-bag-labels-guide/">coffee bag guide</a> covers a different package and handling path. Send the bottle, filled photo and cold-chain details through the <a href="/get-quote/">quote form</a>. The label should still look intentional when the bottle is ready to drink.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'product-instruction-stickers-guide'
    Title = 'Product Instruction Stickers: Put the First Step Where It Is Needed'
    Description = 'Plan removable or permanent product instruction stickers around setup order, icon clarity, safe placement, surface testing and approved wording.'
    Category = 'Product setup labels'
    CardDescription = 'The best quick-start label helps at the moment the product is first handled.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of an unbranded countertop device with a complete original illustrated three-step setup sticker beside its paper manual'
    Caption = 'A short original icon sequence can guide the first action without replacing the full manual.'
    Published = '2026-10-07'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can an instruction sticker replace the product manual?', 'Not automatically. The product owner must decide what instructions and warnings belong on the product, in the manual and on packaging for each market.'),
      @('Should a quick-start sticker be removable?', 'That depends on whether the information is needed only at first setup or throughout use. Test removal on the finished product if clean peel is required.'),
      @('Can generic icons be used for safety steps?', 'Use original, unambiguous illustrations approved by the product owner. Safety-critical symbols and wording need qualified review; decorative icons alone are not instructions.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A new product can arrive with a perfectly good manual still inside the box. The first action happens before the owner reads it: they reach for a switch, remove a film or try to attach a part. A small instruction sticker can put the next step on the surface they are already looking at. It should not pretend to replace the full instructions.</p>
  <p>I would write the sticker around one moment, not the whole product lifecycle. Is it a first-use peel reminder, a three-step assembly cue or a permanent cleaning reference? These are different jobs. If the team tries to put all three on one label, type shrinks and the decision becomes harder.</p>
  <h2>Choose the Action Before the Icon</h2>
  <p>Write the action in plain words, then draw original icons that support it. A numbered sequence can be clearer than three unrelated symbols. Print it at the final size and ask someone who has not seen the product to point to step one. If they hesitate, simplify the copy or placement. Do not use a familiar safety pictogram for a different meaning just because it looks official.</p>
  <h2>Placement Can Create a New Problem</h2>
  <p>Check whether the sticker covers a vent, serial plate, control or required warning. Keep it away from surfaces that become hot or move during operation unless the product owner has approved a suitable construction. For a removable first-use label, trial the intended dwell time and peel on the production finish. Powder coating, glossy plastic and textured housing may not behave alike.</p>
  <p>Sometimes a printed insert wins. If the instruction needs many steps, translations or safety detail, an insert gives it more room and may prevent a crowded product face. The sticker earns its place when one short action at the point of use prevents a predictable mistake.</p>
  <h2>Approve the Real First-Use Scene</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Define the single moment the label should help</li><li>Use owner-approved wording and original icons</li><li>Proof the sequence at final size</li><li>Place it clear of controls and required markings</li><li>Test adhesion or removal on the final finish</li><li>Keep the manual and label versions in sync</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">removable adhesive guide</a> frames the peel decision, while the <a href="/blog/custom-electronics-serial-number-labels-guide/">serial label guide</a> helps protect permanent identifiers. Send product photos and the approved first-use steps through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. A useful sticker leaves the product easier to start, not harder to understand.</p>
'@
  }
)
